y line can contain at most one of \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); the fourth point \\((3,1)\\) cannot be paired with any of them by a sunny line.\n   - \\(y=2\\) leaves \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\). The point \\((1,1)\\) can only be paired sunny‑ly with \\((2,2)\\) (already on the non‑sunny line), leaving \\((1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) which cannot be covered by a single sunny line.\n   - \\(x+y=3\\) leaves \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\). The only sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and an uncovered point is \\(y=x\\) (through \\((2,2)\\)), leaving \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) which are joined by a slope \\(-1\\) line (non‑sunny).  \n6. In every possible selection of the single non‑sunny line we reach a contradiction, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  All lines determined by two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\nFor each unordered pair \\(\\{p,q\\}\\subset\\mathcal P\\) compute the slope.\n\n| Pair | Equation | Slope |\n|------|----------|-------|\n| \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) | \\(y=x\\) | \\(1\\) (sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\(y=-x+3\\) | \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\(y=2\\) | \\(0\\) (non‑sunny) |\n| \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) | \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) | \