\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=3\\). Contradiction.\n\nThus the non‑sunny line cannot contain three points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑sunny line containing exactly two points\n\nThe remaining candidates in (1) are  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad x+y=3 .\n\\]\n\nWe treat each separately.\n\n---\n\n**(i)  Non‑sunny line \\(x=2\\).**  \nIt covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nUncovered points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n- Any sunny line through two of \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) would have to be vertical, which is non‑sunny.  \n- The only sunny line that can contain \\((3,1)\\) together with one of the three points on the \\(x=1\\) column is the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  This line leaves \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) uncovered, and they cannot be joined by a sunny line (the line \\(x=1\\) is non‑sunny).  \nHence two sunny lines cannot cover the four points.\n\n---\n\n**(ii)  Non‑sunny line \\(y=2\\).**  \nIt covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \nUncovered points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n- The point \\((1,1)\\) can be paired sunny‑ly only with \\((2,2)\\) (already on the non‑sunny line) via the line \\(y=x\\).  \n  Thus one sunny line must be \\(y=x\\), covering \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n- The remaining points \\((1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) must be covered by the second sunny line.  \n  Any line through two of them is either slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny) or horizontal (non‑sunny).  \n  Consequently they cannot be covered by a single sunny line.  \nThus this case is impossible.\n\n---\n\n**(iii)  Non‑sunny line \\(x+y=3\\).**  \nIt covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nUncovered points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n- The only sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and an uncovered point is \\(y=x\\) (through \\((2,2)\\)).  \n  Hence one sunny line is \\(y=x\\).  \n- The points left uncovered are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The line through them has equation \\(x+y