le n\\) delete the sunny line \\(\\ell_j'\\) and insert the vertical line \\(x=j\\).\n\nWe must check that after this replacement every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered.\n\n*Points belonging to a retained sunny line* are obviously covered.  \n\n*Points belonging to a deleted sunny line \\(\\ell_j'\\) (with \\(j>k\\))* lie in the pair \\(P_j'\\).  \nThe pair \\(P_j'\\) consists of the two points \\((j,\\,n+2-j)\\) and \\((j+1,\\,n-j)\\).  \nBoth of these points lie on the vertical line \\(x=j\\) (the first one) or on the vertical line \\(x=j+1\\) (the second one).  \nSince we have inserted the vertical line \\(x=j\\) and also the vertical line \\(x=j+1\\) (the latter will be inserted when we later treat the index \\(j+1\\) unless \\(j=n\\), in which case the second point is \\((n+1,\\,n-n)= (n+1,0)\\) and does not belong to \\(S_n\\)), each of the two points is covered by one of the newly added vertical lines.\n\nThus after the replacement all points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered, and we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(L_1,L_2\\).  All other \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. each is either\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\);\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) (equivalently a line \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** In any column \\(x=c\\) the points that are *not* on the vertical line \\(x=c\\) can be covered by at most **four** of the remaining lines: at most one point by \\(L_1\\), at most one point by \\(L_2\\), at most one point by a horizontal line, and at most one point by a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A horizontal line meets the column in at most one point, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) also meets the column in at most one point.  Each sunny line meets the column in at most one point because it is not vertical.  ∎\n\n---\n\nConsider the first column \\(x=1\\).  It contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).\n\nIf the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal C\\), then by Lemma 1 at most four of those \\(n\\) points can be covered – impossible because \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the line \\(x=1\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nDelete the line \\(x=1\\) and all points of the first column.  The remaining points form exactly the set \\(S_{n-1}\\); the remaining lines consist of the two sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) and \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, one of which is the vertical line \\(x=1\\) that we have just removed.  Consequently we obtain a configuration for the integer \\(n-1\\) that still contains **exactly two sunny lines**.\n\nRepeating the same argument we deduce that for every integer \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m\\le n\\) a configuration with two sunny lines would have to contain the vertical line \\(x=1\\); after deleting it we would obtain a configuration for \\(m-1\\) with two sunny lines.  By induction we would finally obtain a configuration with two sunny lines for \\(m=3\\).\n\nA direct exhaustive check for \\(n=3\\) (the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\)) shows that no choice of three distinct lines can contain exactly two sunny lines while covering all six points (the only possibilities are the three cases \\(k=0,1,3\\) exhibited in the introduction).  Hence a configuration with two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThe inductive reduction therefore yields a contradiction for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any }n\\ge3.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\);\n* \\(k=1\\);\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nWe have proved that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**  "
