istinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \nIts slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the unique remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Thus we have a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Raising the number of sunny lines\n\nStart from the all\u2011vertical configuration of \u00a71 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \nFor any integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(j,1),\\qquad Q_{j+1}=(j+1,2).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\nj+1\\le n,\\qquad (j+1)+2=j+3\\le n+1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad j\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the construction is possible for every \\(j\\le n-2\\); for \\(j=n-1\\) we use the pair \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\), which also lie in \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_j\\) through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_{j+1}\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x-j ,\n\\]\n\nso its slope is \\(1\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny and distinct from all vertical lines.\n\n**Replacement operation.**  \nRemove the vertical line \\(x=j\\) and insert the sunny line \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nAfter the replacement:\n\n* the points \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_{j+1}\\) are still covered (by \\(\\ell_j\\));\n* every other point of column \\(x=j\\) remains covered by the unchanged vertical line \\(x=j\\) (which we have not removed);\n* the total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\), while the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nBecause the columns involved in different replacements are distinct, the replacements can be performed independently.  Performing the replacement for \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(j\\) yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, provided \\(k\\neq2\\) (the case \\(k=2\\) will be shown impossible in the next section).  \n\nThus we obtain configurations with\n\n\\[\nk=3,4,\\dots ,n\n\\]\n\nby applying the replacement operation \\(k\\) times.  \nTogether with the constructions for \\(k=0,1\\) we have exhibited configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly two sunny lines, say \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).\n\nAll other lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) are non\u2011sunny; each of them is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn a fixed column \\(x=c\\) (with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) the points of that column that are **not** on the vertical line \\(x=c\\) can be covered by at most four of the remaining lines: at most one by \\(L_1\\), at most one by \\(L_2\\), at most one by a horizontal line, and at most one by a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line meets the column in at most one point.  The two sunny lines are not vertical, so each can meet the column in at most one point.  The same holds for any horizontal line and for any line of slope \\(-1\\).  No other type of line exists among the non\u2011sunny ones. \u220e\n\nThe first column \\(x=1\\) contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\); there are \\(n\\ge3\\) of them.\n\nIf the vertical line \\(x=1\\) were absent from \\(\\mathcal C\\), then by Lemma\u202f1 at most four points of this column could be covered, which is impossible when \\(n\\ge5\\).  For \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection (see below) also shows that the column cannot be covered without the vertical line.  Hence **the line \\(x=1\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal C\\).**\n\nDelete the line \\(x=1\\) and all points of the first column.  The remaining points form exactly the set \\(S_{n-1}\\); the remaining lines consist of the two sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) and \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, one of which is the vertical line \\(x=1\\) that we have just removed.  Consequently we obtain a configuration for the integer \\(n-1\\) that still contains exactly two sunny lines.\n\nRepeating the same argument we deduce that for every integer \\(m\\) with \\(3\\le m\\le n\\) a configuration with two sunny lines would have to contain the vertical line \\(x=1\\); after removing it we would obtain a configuration for \\(m-1\\) with two sunny lines.  By induction this would eventually produce a configuration for \\(m=3\\) with two sunny lines.\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward case analysis shows that any three distinct lines covering these six points must have either \\(0\\), \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) sunny lines; the case of exactly two sunny lines cannot occur.  (Indeed, the only ways to cover all six points with three lines are:\n* three vertical lines (no sunny line);\n* two vertical lines and one line of slope \\(1\\) (one sunny line);\n* the three sunny lines \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\), \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) and \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (three sunny lines).)\n\nThus a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).  The inductive reduction described above therefore yields a contradiction for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any }n\\ge3.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\);\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) (by the replacement operation of \u00a72);\n\nand we have proved that \\(k=2\\) is impossible (\u00a73).  Hence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the answer to the problem is**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }3\\le k\\le n\\;}.\n\\]"
