points.\n\nc = -5: a = 2b -5. b=3 => a=1, (1,3). b=4 => a=3, (3,4) sum 7 >6. So only (1,3). So not enough.\n\nThus slope 1/2 lines cannot have 3 points.\n\nSlope -2 (p=-2,q=1). Equation: -2a - b = c? Actually slope -2: y = -2x + c => b = -2a + c => 2a + b = c. So points satisfy 2a + b = c. For integer c. Let's try c = 5: 2a + b = 5. Solutions: a=1 => b=3 (1,3). a=2 => b=1 (2,1). a=3 => b=-1 no. So (1,3),(2,1) are in P_5. That's 2 points.\n\nc = 6: 2a + b = 6 => a=1 => b=4 (1,4), a=2 => b=2 (2,2), a=3 => b=0 no. So (1,4),(2,2) are in P_5. That's 2 points.\n\nc = 7: 2a + b = 7 => a=1 => b=5 (1,5), a=2 => b=3 (2,3), a=3 => b=1 (3,1). All three are in P_5: (1,5) sum 6, (2,3) sum 5, (3,1) sum 4. So line y = -2x + 7 passes through (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). That's 3 points! So we have a line with slope -2 covering 3 points.\n\nSimilarly, slope -2 line with c=8: 2a + b = 8 => a=1 => b=6 (1,6) out of range, a=2 => b=4 (2,4) sum 6, a=3 => b=2 (3,2) sum 5, a=4 => b=0 no. So (2,4),(3,2) are in P_5, only 2 points.\n\nThus we have at least one line with slope -2 covering 3 points: L1: y = -2x + 7 covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need 4 more lines covering the remaining 12 points, each covering 3 points. Let's list points covered by L1: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Remove them.\n\nRemaining points:\n\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n(2,1),(2,2),(2,4)\n(3,2),(3,3)\n(4,1),(4,2)\n(5,1)\n\nAlso (2,5?) No, (2,5) sum 7 >6. (3,4) sum 7 >6. (4,3) sum 7 >6. (5,2) sum 7 >6.\n\nNow we need to cover these 12 points with 4 lines, each covering 3 points. Let's try to find lines.\n\nWe already have line y = x covering (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). That's 3 points. Good. So L2: y = x.\n\nRemaining points after L2: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 9 points.\n\nWe need 3 lines covering 9 points, each covering 3 points.\n\nConsider line y = -x + 5? That's slope -1, not allowed. So cannot use slope -1.\n\nConsider line y = -2x + 9? 2a + b = 9 => a=1 => b=7 out of range, a=2 => b=5 (2,5) out, a=3