> a=1, (1,2) in set. b=3 => a=3, (3,3) used. b=4 => a=5, (5,4) out. So (1,2) only.\n\nConsider slope -3 (p=-3,q=1). Equation: -3a - b = c => 3a + b = -c. For c negative? Let's try 3a + b = 7 => a=1 => b=4 (1,4) in set. a=2 => b=1 (2,1) in set. a=3 => b=-2 out. So line y = -3x + 7 passes through (1,4),(2,1). That's 2 points.\n\nConsider slope 1/3? Already considered.\n\nThus it's not obvious to find a covering with 5 lines each covering 3 points. But maybe there is a known construction. Let's try to find a covering manually using a systematic approach.\n\nWe need to partition the 15 points into 5 lines, each line with 3 points, slopes not 0,∞,-1. Let's try to find a set of 5 lines that partition the points.\n\nWe already have L1: y = -2x + 7 covering (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). L2: y = x covering (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). That leaves 9 points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Let's try to find lines covering these.\n\nWe can try to find a line with slope -2 covering (2,4),(3,2),(4,0) out, so only 2 points. Not enough.\n\nMaybe we can use slope -3 covering (1,4),(2,1) as we saw. That's 2 points.\n\nMaybe we can use slope -1/2 covering (1,2),(3,3) but (3,3) used. So not good.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 2 covering (2,1),(3,3) but (3,3) used.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 1 covering (1,2),(2,3) but (2,3) used in L1. So not good.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 1 covering (1,3),(2,4) both in set. That's 2 points.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 1 covering (1,4),(2,5) out.\n\nMaybe we can use slope 1 covering (2,1),(3,2) both in set. That's 2 points.\n\nWe need lines with 3 points. Let's search for lines with slope -2 that can cover 3 points among the remaining set. The line y = -2x + 9 passes through (4,1) and (3,3) used, (2,5) out, (1,7) out. So only (4,1). Not enough.\n\nLine y = -2x + 8 passes through (2,4),(3,2). That's 2 points. Good.\n\nLine y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2) used, (3,0) out. So only (1,4). Not enough.\n\nLine y = -2x + 5 passes through (1,3),(2,1). That's