ines would have to be the \\((n-1)\\) distinct lines\n   \\(y=x+c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{-\\frac{n-2}2,\\dots ,\\frac{n-2}2\\}\\).\n\n   *Contradiction.*  \n   The line \\(y=x\\) (with \\(c=0\\)) already uses up the unique integer \\(c\\) that\n   yields \\(\\frac n2\\) points; any other line \\(y=x+c\\) with \\(c\\neq0\\) contains\n   strictly fewer than \\(\\frac n2\\) points of \\(S\\) because the condition\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces the maximal admissible \\(a\\) to be\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor<\\frac n2\\).  \n   Hence it is impossible for all \\(n-1\\) sunny lines to achieve the bound,\n   and some point of \\(S\\) would remain uncovered.  \n\n   Therefore no configuration exists for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\nCombining steps\u202f1\u20136 we have completely determined the admissible values of\n\\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**The final answer is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;,\\;\\text{all odd }k\\text{ with }1\\le k\\le n-2,\\;\n\\text{and }k=n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is odd.}\\;}\n\\]"

