: y = (2/3)x + 4/3. Check integer points: x=1 => y=2, x=4 => y = (8/3) + 4/3 = 12/3 = 4, (4,4) sum=8 >6, out. So only (1,2).\n\nTry line slope -3/2: (1,4),(2,1) slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, not -3/2. Actually slope -3 is allowed. Already considered.\n\nTry line slope -4/3: (1,4),(4,0) out.\n\nTry line slope -1/4: (1,4),(5,3) slope = (3-4)/(5-1) = -1/4, line y = -0.25 x + c => 4 = -0.25*1 + c => c = 4.25 = 17/4. So line: y = -0.25 x + 17/4. Check integer points: x=1 => y=4, x=5 => y = -1.25 + 4.25 = 3, (5,3) sum=8 >6, out. So only (1,4).\n\nThus it's not easy to find triples for the remaining points. Perhaps there is no solution for n=5, k=5. Let's test if k=n for odd n is indeed possible. For n=3, we found a solution. For n=5, we haven't found one yet. Let's try to see if it's possible at all. The bound per line is floor((k+1)/2) = floor((5+1)/2) = 3. So each line can have at most 3 points. To cover 15 points with 5 lines, each must have exactly 3 points. So we need a partition of the 15 points into 5 triples, each collinear with a sunny slope. Is that possible? Let's try to find a systematic approach.\n\nWe can try to use a known combinatorial design: The points form a triangular grid. We need to partition into 5 lines of slope not 0, ∞, -1. This is reminiscent of a \"Steiner triple system\" but with geometry constraints. Let's see if there is a known solution. Perhaps the answer k=n for odd n is not correct for n≥5. The solution's claim might be wrong. Let's test n=5 more thoroughly.\n\nWe can try to use a computer search (but we cannot do that manually). However, we can reason about parity constraints. Each line of slope not 0, ∞, -1 can have at most 3 points. So each line must have exactly 3 points. Let's consider the parity of a+b for points. Points in the triangle have a+b ranging from 2 to 6. The parity of a+b alternates: sum even for (a+b) even, odd for odd. For a line of slope 1 (y = x + c), the sum a+b = 2a + c, which has parity same as c (since 2a is even). So for a line of slope 1, all points have the same parity of sum (equal to parity of c). For a line of slope -2 (y = -2x + c), sum a+b = a + (-2a + c) = -a + c, parity depends on a and c. So parity can vary.\n\nBut maybe there is a parity argument that prevents covering all points with 5 lines each of size 3. Let's examine the total number of points with each parity. For n=5, points (a,b) with a+b ≤ 6. Let's count points with even sum and odd sum.\n\nList points:\n\nSum=2 (even): (1,1) -> 1 point.\nSum=3 (odd): (1,2),(2,1) -> 2 points.\nSum=4 (even): (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) -> 3 points.\nSum=5 (odd): (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) -> 4 points.\nSum=6 (even): (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1) -> 5 points.\n\nTotal even sum points: 1+3+5 = 9. Total odd sum points: 2+4 = 6. So there are 9 even-sum points, 6 odd-sum points.\n\nNow consider a sunny line. For slope 1 lines, all points have same parity (c parity). For slope -1 lines (forbidden). For other slopes, parity can vary. But maybe each sunny line can contain at most 2 points of the same parity? Let's examine.\n\nTake a line with slope p/q (reduced). Points (a,b) satisfy p a - q b = constant. Then a+b parity? Not obvious.\n\nBut maybe we can find a parity argument that shows k=n for odd n > 3 is impossible. Let's try to see if any known solution exists for n=5, k=5. If not, the answer's claim that k=n when n odd is false for n=5. But we need to verify.\n\nLet's try to search manually for a partition of the 15 points into 5 triples. We can try to systematically generate triples of points that are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nFirst, we can list all possible triples of points in the triangle that are collinear with a sunny slope. Then see if we can cover all points with 5 disjoint triples.\n\nWe can try to find all triples of points (a,b) with a+b ≤ 6 that are collinear and not on a forbidden line. Let's enumerate possible slopes.\n\nWe can consider lines determined by any two points, then see if a third point lies on the same line. For each pair of points, compute slope and see if there is a third point.\n\nWe can try to find triples manually.\n\nList all points again:\n\n1. (1,1)\n2. (1,2)\n3. (1,3)\n4. (1,4)\n5. (1,5)\n6. (2,1)\n7. (2,2)\n8. (2,3)\n9. (2,4)\n10. (3,1)\n11. (3,2)\n12. (3,3)\n13. (4,1)\n14. (4,2)\n15. (5,1)\n\nNow find triples.\n\nWe already have triple (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) slope 1.\n\nTriple (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) slope -2.\n\nTriple (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) slope -1/2 (we found earlier). Indeed (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) are collinear: slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, and (5,1) also lies: (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2. So triple (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) works.\n\nTriple (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) out. So not.\n\nTriple (1,4),(3,3),(5,2) out because (5,2) not in set.\n\nTriple (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) out.\n\nTriple (2,1),(3,3),(4,5) out.\n\nTriple (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) out.\n\nTriple (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) out because (4,3) sum=7 >6.\n\nTriple (2,1),(5,1) vertical line x=2? Actually (2,1) and (5,1) are horizontal? No, they have same y=1, so horizontal line y=1, which is forbidden (non-sunny). So not allowed.\n\nTriple (2,1),(4,1) horizontal line y=1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (2,1),(5,1) also horizontal.\n\nTriple (1,2),(2,1) slope = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,2),(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, line y = -0.5 x + 2.5. Check if any third point: (5,0) out. So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,2),(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, line y = -1/3 x + 5/3? Actually compute: y = -1/3 x + c => 2 = -1/3*1 + c => c = 2 + 1/3 = 7/3. So line: y = -1/3 x + 7/3. Check integer points: x=1 => y=2, x=4 => y = -4/3 + 7/3 = 1, (4,1) is there. x=7 => y = -7/3 + 7/3 = 0 (out). So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,2),(5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4, line y = -0.25 x + c => 2 = -0.25*1 + c => c = 2.25 = 9/4. So line: y = -0.25 x + 9/4. Check integer points: x=1 => y=2, x=5 => y = -1.25 + 2.25 = 1, (5,1) is there. x=9 => y = -2.25 + 2.25 = 0 (out). So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,3),(2,2) slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,3),(2,4) slope = (4-3)/(2-1) = 1, slope 1, line y = x + 2. Points: (1,3),(2,4),(3,5) out. So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,3),(4,2) slope = (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3, line y = -1/3 x + c => 3 = -1/3*1 + c => c = 3 + 1/3 = 10/3. So line: y = -1/3 x + 10/3. Check integer points: x=1 => y=3, x=4 => y = -4/3 + 10/3 = 2, (4,2) is there. x=7 => y = -7/3 + 10/3 = 1, (7,1) out. So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,3),(5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -1/2, line y = -0.5 x + c => 3 = -0.5*1 + c => c = 3.5 = 7/2. So line: y = -0.5 x + 7/2. Check integer points: x=1 => y=3, x=3 => y = -1.5 + 3.5 = 2, (3,2) is there, x=5 => y = -2.5 + 3.5 = 1, (5,1) is there. So triple (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) we already have.\n\nTriple (1,4),(2,3) slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,4),(3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,4),(4,1) slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,4),(5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-1) = -3/4, line y = -0.75 x + c => 4 = -0.75*1 + c => c = 4.75 = 19/4. Check integer points: x=1 => y=4, x=5 => y = -3.75 + 4.75 = 1, (5,1) is there. x=9 => y = -6.75 + 4.75 = -2 (out). So only two points.\n\nTriple (1,5),(2,4) slope = (4-5)/(2-1) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,5),(3,3) slope = (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,5),(4,2) slope = (2-5)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (1,5),(5,1) slope = (1-5)/(5-1) = -4/4 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (2,2),(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (2,2),(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, line y = -0.5 x + c => 2 = -0.5*2 + c => c = 3. So line: y = -0.5 x + 3. Check integer points: x=2 => y=2, x=4 => y = -2 + 3 = 1, (4,1) is there. x=6 => y = -3 + 3 = 0 (out). So points: (2,2),(4,1). Only two.\n\nTriple (2,2),(5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, line y = -1/3 x + c => 2 = -1/3*2 + c => c = 2 + 2/3 = 8/3. So line: y = -1/3 x + 8/3. Check integer points: x=2 => y=2, x=5 => y = -5/3 + 8/3 = 1, (5,1) is there. x=8 => y = -8/3 + 8/3 = 0 (out). So only two.\n\nTriple (2,3),(3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, line y = -2x + c => 3 = -2*2 + c => c = 7. So line: y = -2x + 7. Points: (2,3),(3,1). Check x=4 => y = -8 + 7 = -1 (out). So only two.\n\nTriple (2,3),(4,1) slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (2,3),(5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, line y = -2/3 x + c => 3 = -2/3*2 + c => c = 3 + 4/3 = 13/3. So line: y = -2/3 x + 13/3. Check integer points: x=2 => y=3, x=5 => y = -10/3 + 13/3 = 1, (5,1) is there. x=8 => y = -16/3 + 13/3 = -1 (out). So only two.\n\nTriple (2,4),(3,2) slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, line y = -2x + c => 4 = -2*2 + c => c = 8. So line: y = -2x + 8. Points: (2,4),(3,2). x=4 => y = -8 + 8 = 0 (out). So only two.\n\nTriple (2,4),(4,2) slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (2,4),(5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (3,1),(4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, slope 1, line y = x - 2? Actually line through (3,1) and (4,2) has slope 1, equation y = x - 2. Points: (3,1),(4,2),(5,3) out. So only two.\n\nTriple (3,1),(5,1) horizontal, forbidden.\n\nTriple (3,2),(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (3,2),(5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2, line y = -0.5 x + c => 2 = -0.5*3 + c => c = 2 + 1.5 = 3.5 = 7/2. So line: y = -0.5 x + 7/2. Check integer points: x=3 => y=2, x=5 => y = -2.5 + 3.5 = 1, (5,1) is there. x=7 => y = -3.5 + 3.5 = 0 (out). So only two.\n\nTriple (3,3),(4,2) slope = (2-3)/(4-3) = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (3,3),(5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, forbidden.\n\nTriple (4,1),(5,1) horizontal, forbidden.\n\nThus the only triples we found are:\n\n- (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) slope 1.\n- (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) slope -2.\n- (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) slope -1/2.\n\nWe need 5 triples, but we only have 3 triples. Are there any other triples we missed? Let's check other possibilities.\n\nWe haven't considered triples with points (2,1),(3,3),(4,5) out. (2,1),(4,2),(6,3) out. (2,1),(5,1) horizontal, forbidden. (2,1),(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, line y = x - 1? Actually line through (2,1) and (3,2) has slope 1, equation y = x - 1. Points: (2,1),(3,2