 full coverage.*  \n   Every original point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) falls into one of three mutually exclusive cases:\n\n   * \\(a\\le v\\): covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (2);\n   * \\(b\\le h\\): covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (3);\n   * \\(a\\ge v+1,\\;b\\ge h+1\\): after the shift \\((a',b')\\) belongs to \\(T_k\\) and is covered by a line \\(L_i\\) as shown above.\n\n   Hence all required points are covered and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines (the \\(L_i\\)\u2019s) are sunny.\n\n5. **The extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) (when \\(n\\) is odd).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\). No line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd.*  \n   Write \\(n=2r+1\\). Use exactly the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) from (4) with \\(v=h=0\\). The same argument as in step\u202f4 shows that they cover the whole triangle \\(\\{(a,b):a+b\\le n+1\\}\\). Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists precisely when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n6. **Non\u2011existence of any other values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Even \\(k\\ge2\\)* \u2013 excluded by step\u202f3.  \n\n   *Odd \\(k\\) with \\(k>n-2\\) and \\(k\\neq n\\).*  \n   If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\ge n-1\\) but \\(k\\neq n\\), then \\(n-k\\le1\\).  \n   \u2013 When \\(n-k=1\\) there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line. A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of the required set (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) lines each intersect the set in at most \\(n\\) integer points).  \n   \u2013 By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) points.  \n   A short counting argument shows that the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n+\\bigl(k\\bigr)\\frac{n}{2}\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n   \\]\n\n   with equality forcing all sunny lines to have slope \\(1\\) and to be pairwise disjoint. This can happen only when \\(k=n\\) (the construction of step\u202f5). Hence no configuration exists for odd \\(k\\) with \\(n-1\\ge k\\ge n-2\\) unless \\(k=n\\).\n\n   Combining all the above, the list in the statement is exhaustive.\n\n---\n\n**The final answer is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;,\\;\\text{all odd }k\\text{ with }1\\le k\\le n-2,\\;\n\\text{and }k=n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is odd.}\\;}\n\\]"
